# Architecture first

Many home-automation projects begin with a platform:

> “This is a Control4 house.”

> “This is a Crestron project.”

> “This is an Alexa home.”

We prefer a different sequence.

## Requirements first  
## Architecture second  
## Products and platforms third

A proprietary platform may be the correct solution.

It should not be the question the project starts with.

We first ask:

- How does the homeowner want to live?
- What should happen automatically?
- What infrastructure does the house need?
- What happens when the internet is unavailable?
- What data leaves the home?
- Who owns the accounts?
- Who can service the system?
- What happens if a manufacturer changes direction?
- Can one subsystem be replaced without rebuilding everything else?

Only then do we select the technology.

---

# Platform-centric vs. architecture-first

| Platform-centric approach | Architecture-first approach |
|---|---|
| Starts with a proprietary ecosystem | Starts with the homeowner, house, and build plan |
| Fits the project into platform capabilities | Defines requirements first, then selects systems |
| Owns the automation system | Owns dependencies between technology scopes |
| Future changes follow the platform and dealer lifecycle | Infrastructure preserves options and reduces retrofit risk |
| One ecosystem becomes the center of the home | The home remains the center; platforms are selected where appropriate |

The objective is not ideological openness.

The objective is **lifecycle resilience**.

---

# The homeowner should own the home

That includes the technology.

Where practical, the homeowner should receive:

- administrative credentials;
- account ownership;
- system documentation;
- network documentation;
- device inventories;
- integration notes;
- service information;
- backup and recovery information.

A future homeowner should not inherit an expensive house and then have to reverse-engineer:
- who controls the system;
- which dealer has access;
- what depends on the cloud;
- what requires a subscription;
- what can be changed independently.

**The house should outlive today's devices, platforms, and service relationships.**

---

# Privacy is part of the architecture

Connected homes can collect sensitive information:
- camera footage
- occupancy patterns
- door activity
- device usage
- environmental data
- access credentials
- remote-control history

Privacy should therefore not be treated as a checkbox added after installation.

It can affect the architecture itself.

Depending on the project, that may include:
- local control;
- network segmentation;
- controlled remote access;
- limited cloud dependency;
- clear account ownership;
- documented service access;
- security-aware device selection.

> **A modern home should not become less private simply because it becomes more capable.**

---

# A better-designed home gives attention back

The goal of home technology is not to give people more technology to manage.
It is the opposite.

A well-designed home should reduce:
- repetitive decisions;
- unnecessary notifications;
- multiple apps;
- disconnected controls;
- battery maintenance;
- Wi-Fi troubleshooting;
- manual routines.

Lighting, climate, shades, security, energy, audio, and connectivity should behave as one environment.

> **Your home should not take your attention.**

## Less home management. More life.

